1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,298
The asteroid that killed the dinosaurs was about ten kilometers wide.

2
00:00:04,298 --> 00:00:07,683
The ocean, on average, is less than four kilometers deep.

3
00:00:07,683 --> 00:00:15,048
So if a rock like that hit the open sea, it would touch the seafloor while its back end was still sticking out of the water.

4
00:00:15,747 --> 00:00:18,128
Seventy one percent of Earth is ocean.

5
00:00:18,128 --> 00:00:21,135
So most space rocks that reach us land in water.

6
00:00:21,135 --> 00:00:22,702
Today we run a drop test.

7
00:00:22,702 --> 00:00:26,085
Same ocean, five rocks, each one bigger than the last.

8
00:00:26,085 --> 00:00:31,598
And we'll find out where the water saves us, and where it makes things much, much worse.

9
00:00:32,298 --> 00:00:34,154
What If an Asteroid Hit the Ocean?

10
00:00:34,854 --> 00:00:35,422
Rock one.

11
00:00:35,422 --> 00:00:36,874
Eighteen meters across.

12
00:00:36,874 --> 00:00:39,398
About the size of a five story building.

13
00:00:39,398 --> 00:00:43,942
This is the kind of rock that hits Earth's atmosphere every few decades.

14
00:00:44,642 --> 00:00:48,498
We know exactly what this one does, because it already happened.

15
00:00:48,498 --> 00:00:53,800
In February twenty thirteen, a rock about this size came in over Chelyabinsk, in Russia.

16
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:55,487
It never reached the ground.

17
00:00:55,487 --> 00:01:04,162
Air pressure tore it apart about thirty kilometers up, releasing roughly four hundred forty kilotons of energy, thirty times the Hiroshima bomb.

18
00:01:04,862 --> 00:01:13,383
Over a city, that blast shattered windows across a whole region and injured about fifteen hundred people, most of them from flying glass.

19
00:01:13,383 --> 00:01:17,612
Over the open ocean, the same explosion would leave almost no trace.

20
00:01:17,612 --> 00:01:18,980
No crater, no tsunami.

21
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:21,592
Just a flash and a boom that nobody hears.

22
00:01:21,592 --> 00:01:28,371
And since most of the planet is water, most rocks like this, statistically, explode where nobody is watching.

23
00:01:29,072 --> 00:01:29,591
Rock two.

24
00:01:29,591 --> 00:01:31,149
A hundred and forty meters.

25
00:01:31,149 --> 00:01:35,130
Taller than the Statue of Liberty, and smaller than the Eiffel Tower.

26
00:01:35,830 --> 00:01:38,958
This size matters, because it's the line NASA drew.

27
00:01:38,958 --> 00:01:43,865
Rocks of a hundred forty meters and up are the ones Congress asked NASA to find.

28
00:01:43,865 --> 00:01:49,937
There are about twenty five thousand of them near Earth, and we've found only around forty percent.

29
00:01:49,937 --> 00:01:53,985
On land, NASA says, one of these could devastate an entire region.

30
00:01:54,685 --> 00:01:56,179
This one doesn't burn up.

31
00:01:56,179 --> 00:02:05,141
It punches straight through the air and into the sea, and for a moment, it opens a hole in the ocean, a crater made of water, several kilometers wide.

32
00:02:05,141 --> 00:02:12,669
Then the walls of that hole collapse, and the water rushes back in, collides in the middle, and shoots up in a tower of spray.

33
00:02:13,369 --> 00:02:14,785
And here's the surprise.

34
00:02:14,785 --> 00:02:16,496
You'd expect a giant tsunami.

35
00:02:16,496 --> 00:02:24,165
But in computer simulations at Los Alamos, the physicist Galen Gisler found that waves from impacts like this are short and steep.

36
00:02:24,165 --> 00:02:28,826
They break and fade in the open ocean, much faster than earthquake tsunamis do.

37
00:02:28,826 --> 00:02:30,005
The danger is local.

38
00:02:30,005 --> 00:02:35,374
If it lands within about a hundred kilometers of a coast, that coast is in serious trouble.

39
00:02:35,374 --> 00:02:38,265
Far out at sea, the waves die before they arrive.

40
00:02:38,965 --> 00:02:39,666
Rock three.

41
00:02:39,666 --> 00:02:41,258
Two hundred fifty meters.

42
00:02:41,258 --> 00:02:43,487
Almost as tall as the Eiffel Tower.

43
00:02:44,187 --> 00:02:51,032
In Gisler's simulations, a rock this size hitting deep water throws up something you can't see in the movies.

44
00:02:51,032 --> 00:02:51,409
Steam.

45
00:02:51,409 --> 00:02:57,564
Up to two hundred fifty million tonnes of water, flashed into vapor and blasted high into the sky.

46
00:02:58,263 --> 00:02:59,481
Why does that matter?

47
00:02:59,481 --> 00:03:04,523
Because water vapor that reaches the stratosphere, above the weather, doesn't rain out.

48
00:03:04,523 --> 00:03:08,696
It can linger there for a long time, and it's a powerful greenhouse gas.

49
00:03:08,696 --> 00:03:14,261
Gisler's own conclusion was that the most significant effect of an ocean impact is not the wave.

50
00:03:14,261 --> 00:03:17,101
It's the steam, with possible effects on climate.

51
00:03:17,801 --> 00:03:18,416
Rock four.

52
00:03:18,416 --> 00:03:19,277
One kilometer.

53
00:03:19,277 --> 00:03:22,046
Now we're past anything a city could survive.

54
00:03:22,746 --> 00:03:26,990
When this one hits the sea, the steam carries something else with it.

55
00:03:26,990 --> 00:03:27,297
Salt.

56
00:03:27,297 --> 00:03:34,309
And sea salt is full of chlorine and bromine, the same kind of chemicals that tore the ozone hole over Antarctica.

57
00:03:35,009 --> 00:03:42,171
In a twenty ten study, Elisabetta Pierazzo and her colleagues modeled a one kilometer asteroid landing in the ocean.

58
00:03:42,171 --> 00:03:51,495
The result was an ozone hole covering much of the planet, with more than seventy percent of the ozone gone at mid to high latitudes for over two years.

59
00:03:51,495 --> 00:03:58,040
The ultraviolet index, which the World Health Organization calls extreme at eleven, could reach fifty six.

60
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:00,139
A number nobody has ever measured.

61
00:04:00,839 --> 00:04:02,163
And this has happened.

62
00:04:02,163 --> 00:04:13,356
About two and a half million years ago, an asteroid somewhere around a kilometer wide fell into the Southern Ocean, off the coast of Chile, where the sea is four to five kilometers deep.

63
00:04:13,356 --> 00:04:15,222
It's called the Eltanin impact.

64
00:04:15,222 --> 00:04:17,870
Scientists found its debris in seafloor mud.

65
00:04:17,870 --> 00:04:19,976
But no clear crater was ever found.

66
00:04:19,976 --> 00:04:21,661
The deep water took the hit.

67
00:04:22,361 --> 00:04:23,022
Rock five.

68
00:04:23,022 --> 00:04:24,013
Ten kilometers.

69
00:04:24,013 --> 00:04:25,731
Taller than Mount Everest.

70
00:04:26,432 --> 00:04:34,016
Sixty six million years ago, a rock this size struck what is now Mexico's Yucatan Peninsula, which was then covered by a shallow sea.

71
00:04:34,016 --> 00:04:38,978
At around twenty kilometers per second, it crossed the water in a fraction of a second.

72
00:04:38,978 --> 00:04:41,430
For a rock this big, the ocean is a puddle.

73
00:04:41,430 --> 00:04:45,707
It hit the seafloor and dug a crater about a hundred fifty kilometers wide.

74
00:04:45,707 --> 00:04:47,931
The water above it didn't slow it down.

75
00:04:47,931 --> 00:04:52,778
It just added to the chaos, because now a whole sea had been thrown out of its basin.

76
00:04:53,478 --> 00:04:55,028
Then the water came back.

77
00:04:55,028 --> 00:05:02,842
In twenty twenty two, a team led by Molly Range at the University of Michigan ran the first global simulation of that tsunami.

78
00:05:02,842 --> 00:05:10,035
Within about ten minutes, a wall of water around one and a half kilometers tall was rolling outward from the crater.

79
00:05:10,035 --> 00:05:15,430
That is almost twice the height of the Burj Khalifa, the tallest building in the world.

80
00:05:16,130 --> 00:05:20,604
Within two days, the waves had reached most of the world's coastlines.

81
00:05:20,604 --> 00:05:31,341
Their energy, the team estimated, was up to thirty thousand times larger than the twenty oh four Indian Ocean tsunami, one of the deadliest disasters in modern history.

82
00:05:32,041 --> 00:05:36,016
In deep water, a tsunami moves about as fast as a jet airliner.

83
00:05:36,016 --> 00:05:43,335
Our own calculation for an ocean three point seven kilometers deep gives close to seven hundred kilometers per hour.

84
00:05:43,335 --> 00:05:51,412
Across open ocean, the Chicxulub waves were still more than ten meters high as they approached the coasts of the North Atlantic.

85
00:05:52,112 --> 00:05:54,006
We may even have its footprints.

86
00:05:54,006 --> 00:06:00,047
In twenty twenty one, geologists found giant ripples buried one and a half kilometers under Louisiana.

87
00:06:00,047 --> 00:06:03,659
Each about sixteen meters tall, and six hundred meters apart.

88
00:06:03,659 --> 00:06:07,686
They think the tsunami's currents carved them into the old seafloor.

89
00:06:07,686 --> 00:06:10,114
Ripples on a beach are a few centimeters.

90
00:06:10,114 --> 00:06:12,719
These are the size of a five story building.

91
00:06:13,420 --> 00:06:15,192
So does the ocean protect us?

92
00:06:15,192 --> 00:06:16,230
Here's the twist.

93
00:06:16,230 --> 00:06:18,614
For almost every rock on our list, yes.

94
00:06:18,614 --> 00:06:24,602
In twenty sixteen, NASA and NOAA brought tsunami experts together to settle exactly this question.

95
00:06:24,602 --> 00:06:35,051
Their conclusion: across all the rocks that could realistically hit us, the danger from tsunamis is substantially smaller than the danger from the same rocks hitting land.

96
00:06:35,752 --> 00:06:41,837
The sea absorbs the blast, the waves break and fade, and most of the planet's water is far from any city.

97
00:06:41,837 --> 00:06:45,256
The one exception is the rock that's too big for any ocean.

98
00:06:45,256 --> 00:06:48,212
And that's exactly the kind we're best at tracking.

99
00:06:48,212 --> 00:06:57,021
Every known asteroid over one kilometer that comes near Earth has been studied, and none of them is on a collision course for at least the next century.

100
00:06:57,721 --> 00:06:59,244
And we can now push back.

101
00:06:59,244 --> 00:07:05,763
In twenty twenty two, NASA flew its DART spacecraft into a small asteroid moon called Dimorphos on purpose.

102
00:07:05,763 --> 00:07:08,992
It shortened that moon's orbit by thirty two minutes.

103
00:07:08,992 --> 00:07:13,804
It was the first time humanity changed the motion of a natural object in space.

104
00:07:13,804 --> 00:07:22,638
And NEO Surveyor, a telescope built to hunt the rocks we haven't found yet, is scheduled to launch no earlier than September twenty twenty seven.

105
00:07:23,338 --> 00:07:24,973
So, five rocks, one ocean.

106
00:07:24,973 --> 00:07:27,300
The small ones never touch the water.

107
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:33,337
The mid-sized ones are dangerous mostly near a coast, and they send steam and salt into the sky.

108
00:07:33,337 --> 00:07:36,921
And the giant one treats the deepest ocean like a puddle.

109
00:07:36,921 --> 00:07:43,776
Seventy one percent of our planet is water, and it is a better shield than it looks, until it suddenly isn't.

110
00:07:44,476 --> 00:07:47,409
Next time, a real milestone, happening this week.

111
00:07:47,409 --> 00:07:54,833
Voyager 1, launched in nineteen seventy seven, is about to become the first human-made object one full light-day from Earth.

112
00:07:54,833 --> 00:07:58,186
A radio message will need twenty four hours to reach it.

113
00:07:58,186 --> 00:08:00,162
We'll put that distance to scale.

114
00:08:00,162 --> 00:08:02,976
Subscribe to Cosmic Scale so you don't miss it.

115
00:08:02,976 --> 00:08:06,748
And tell me in the comments, which rock surprised you the most?
